1.1.text:
Article 3

The objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence are: a. to prevent all forms of violence against women and children; b. to provide protection and services to women and child victims of gender-based violence;

c. to provide a sense of security to women and child victims of violence; d. to restore the physical, psychological and economic condition of women and child victims of violence;

e. best interests of women and child victims of violence that occur in the domestic and/or public sphere; and

f. to take action against perpetrators of violence against women and children.

Article17

(1) To prevent acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government carries out empowerment and awareness to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling and realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.

(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1),the Regional Government undertakes the following efforts:

a. providing material on the prevention of acts of violence against women and children in formal, non-formal, and informal education;
b. providing accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services; c. creating a friendly and safe work environment for women;
d. building community participation and concern for the prevention of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing an easily accessible information system; f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations engaged in and/or concerned with women and children.

Article14

(1) The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 13 letter a, include:

a. establishing policies for the protection and empowerment of women and children;
b. implementing policies, protection, and empowerment of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
c. conducting 10
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children, according to regional financial capacity;
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children; and
g. realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.
(2) In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes programs and action activities for the protection and empowerment of women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing the protection and empowerment of women and children.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), is part of the RPJMD.
(4) Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), are regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article18

(1) Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 17 is carried out by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the field of:

a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. labor;
e. population and civil registration;
f. law;
g. women's empowerment and child protection;
h. cooperatives and micro enterprises;
i. mental and spiritual; and
j. public order and security.
(2) Prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out in an integrated and continuous manner based on the RPJPD and RPJMD.

Article25

(1) The Regional Government establishes partnerships with the business sector in the protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

(2) Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) is conducted through: a. notification of employment opportunity information for women and child victims of violence;

b. education and training for women and child victims of violence;

Article 19 12

Article19

(1) In the framework of protection services for women and children from acts of violence, the Regional Government establishes UPTDPPA.

(2) UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1), functions to implement services:

a. public complaints;
b. victim outreach;
c. case management;
d. temporary shelter;
e. mediation; and
f. victim assistance.
(3) Further provisions regarding the organization and working procedures of UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation.
(4) To assist the protection of women and children, the Regional Government may establish:
a. Task Force for the Prevention and Handling of Criminal Acts of Human Trafficking;
b. Task Force for the Prevention of Child Marriage;
c. Child-Friendly Regency Task Force or by other designation; d. Regional Action Committee for the elimination of the worst forms of child labor;
e. Majalengka Youth Children's Forum;
f. Community-Based Integrated Child Protection (PATBM) GEMPITA.

Article29

(1) Funding for protection of women and child victims of violence includes:

a. medical services, which include doctor's examinations, treatment costs, hospital costs, medication costs and medical support costs;
b. medicolegal services, which include examinations for Visum et Repertum and Visum et Psikiatrikum;
c. psychosocial services, which consist of counseling and psychological therapy as well as safe houses (shelters);
d. legal services;
e. economic empowerment, in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access.
(2) Funding management is carried out through technical institutions appointed by the Regional Government which is conducted transparently and can be legally accounted for.

CHAPTER III

FORMS OF VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND CHILDREN Article 4 Forms of violence against women and children, among others:

a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. exploitation; and/or
f. other violence.

Article 2

Protection of women and children from acts of violence is organized based on the principles of:

a. humanity;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. order
d. order and legal certainty;
e. transparency;
f. protection; and
g. best interests of victims.

Article11

Women and children victims of acts of violence receive the following rights:

a. Right 9
a. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected; b. the right to recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to appropriate, comfortable, and needs-based complaint handling;
h. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process;
i. the right to assistance; and
j. to receive continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 7

Sexual violence as referred to in Article 4 letter c includes: a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;

b. forced sexual intercourse;

c. forced unnatural sexual intercourse; and/or

d. forcing sexual intercourse with another person for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes.

Article12

Child victims of acts of violence in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article11, also receive special rights, as follows: a. the right to survival, growth, and development; b. the right to basic population services;

c. the right to equal protection;

d. the right to be free from various stigma; and

e. the right to obtain freedom.

Article26

(1) Assistance includes all integrated efforts to restore and strengthen the condition of victims, carried out by health workers, education, police, prosecutors, courts, advocates, social workers, volunteer companions and spiritual counselors.

(2) Assistance as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

(3) The duties of companions are:

a. provide sufficient information to victims about their rights;
b. provide psychosocial services to victims so that victims feel safe and comfortable;
c. accompany victims during the medical examination and recovery process;
d. accompany victims during the medicolegal process;
e. accompany victims during the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court;
f. monitor the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court;
g. maintain the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including coverage by mass media;
h. coordinate with other companions; and i. provide continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article24

(1) In order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children, cooperation may be conducted with:

a. Central Government;
b. Provincial Government;
c. Other Regency/City Governments;
d. Non-Governmental Organizations;
e. Community;
f. Higher Education Institutions; and
g. Mass Media.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: a. exchange of data and information;
b. recovery of victims of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration;
d. provision of evidence and witnesses; and
e. followed up in accordance with applicable law.
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be set forth in the form of a Cooperation Agreement in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

